<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  THE PLAY BINARY, MINUS ONE PERMISSION.

  Google Play's Device and Network Abuse policy forbids an app that downloads
  and installs an APK of itself. Playa Pal does exactly that on the sideload
  road ("Update to latest", src/update/appUpdate.ts + AppUpdateModule.kt),
  because a phone that got the app handed to it in the dust has no other
  channel that will ever mention a newer build. On the Play road there IS
  another channel — the Store — so the capability is not merely hidden here,
  it is REMOVED: without REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES the system installer cannot
  be fired at all, whatever any future JS says.

  Hiding alone would not have been enough. A declared permission is what a
  policy scan reads, and "the row is behind a flag" is not a defence for a
  binary that still asks Android for the power. tools:node="remove" strikes
  the line during manifest merge, so the play AAB's merged manifest simply
  does not contain it — verify after any build with:

    ./gradlew :app:processPlayReleaseMainManifest
    grep -c REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES \
      android/app/build/intermediates/merged_manifest/playRelease/AndroidManifest.xml

  ...which must print 0, and the same grep against githubRelease must print 1.

  The Settings rows that USED that power ("Update to latest", "Share Playa
  Pal") are hidden in this flavor by src/config/distribution.ts, which reads
  the generated asset flag this flavor carries. Two independent locks on one
  door, on purpose: the manifest is the one Play scans, the flag is the one a
  camper sees.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES"
        tools:node="remove" />

</manifest>
